th,\"rb\")` → read side → `fclose`), and assert the on-disk bytes are byte-identical between C and Rust **and** that the read-back content equals the original.\n\n| # | entry point(s) | configuration (options set + input shape) | [ ] |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 114 | `LZ4F_writeOpen`, `LZ4F_write`, `LZ4F_writeClose`, `LZ4F_readOpen`, `LZ4F_read`, `LZ4F_readClose` | `prefsPtr = NULL` (⇒ `maxWriteSize = 65536`, `dstBufMaxSize = LZ4F_compressBound(65536, NULL)` i.e. the worst-case NULL-prefs bound). Content `TEXT(200000)` written in **one** `LZ4F_write` call (internally chunked into 65536/65536/65536/3392). Read back in 65536-byte `LZ4F_read` calls. Assert `LZ4F_read` return values, `LZ4F_writeClose`/`LZ4F_readClose` return `LZ4F_OK_NoError`. | [ ] |\n| 115 | `LZ4F_writeOpen`, `LZ4F_write`, `LZ4F_writeClose`, `LZ4F_readOpen`, `LZ4F_read`, `LZ4F_readClose` | `prefsPtr = NULL`; content `TEXT(200000)`; read back **1 byte per `LZ4F_read` call** (200000 calls). Each call drives the internal `while (next < size)` loop through `fread` of up to `srcBufMaxSize = 65536` plus a partial `LZ4F_decompress` with `dstsize = 1` ⇒ heavy `dstage_flushOut` / `tmpOut` traffic and `srcBufNext` bookkeeping. | [ ] |\n| 116 | `LZ4F_writeOpen`, `LZ4F_write`, `LZ4F_writeClose`, `LZ4F_readOpen`, `LZ4F_read`, `LZ4F_readClose` | `prefsPtr = NULL`; content `TEXT(200000)`; read chunk = **100000** (larger than both `srcBufMaxSize` and the 64 KB block) ⇒ two `LZ4F_read` calls each needing multiple `fread` + `LZ4F_decompress` rounds. Also read chunk = 200000 (exactly the whole content in one call) and 300000 (more than the content ⇒ return value must be 200000, hitting the `ret == 0` EOF `break`). | [ ] |\n| 117 | `LZ4F_writeOpen`, `LZ4F_write`, `LZ4F_writeClose`, `LZ4F_readOpen`, `LZ4F_read`, `LZ4F_readClose` | `prefs` = INIT + `blockSizeID = LZ4F_max256KB`, `blockMode = LZ4F_blockLinked`, `level = 0`, `autoFlush = 0` ⇒ `writeOpen`'s switch picks `maxWriteSize = 262144`, `dstBufMaxSize = LZ4F_compressBound