 and all 32 cases produce identical PROCESS-macro sequences.\n\nSince nothing failed, I mutation-tested the suite to prove it has teeth. 14 mutations injected into `src/xxhash.rs` (rotate constants in every PROCESS macro, `>=`/`>` and `<`/`<=` flips in all four loop/branch conditions, `large_len`/`total_len` digest branch selection, `memsize + len < 16` boundary, dropped byte-swaps in both canonical directions, `reset` writing into `reserved`, `copyState` short copies). **12 of 14 were caught**; the 2 survivors were:\n\n- `copyState` copying `sizeof(state) - sizeof(reserved)` instead of the full struct — a **real gap in my test**, which I fixed: `Duo32/64::copy_from` now pre-fills both destination blobs with a *second* sentinel (`0x5A`, distinct from the `0xAA` state sentinel, same value on both sides per the harness rule) so a short copy leaves a detectable tail. Both mutants are now caught.\n- `(len>=16)` → `(len>16)` in `state->large_len |= (len>=16) | (state->total_len_32>=16)` — **provably equivalent**, because `total_len_32` is incremented by `len` on the preceding line so `total_len_32 >= len`; the clause only matters after `total_len_32` wraps at 4 GiB. Documented in a file-header comment rather than tested.\n\n## Notable C behaviours replicated / deliberately skipped\n\n- `XXH_FORCE_ALIGN_CHECK` is 0 on x86_64, so the one-shot entry points always take the `XXH_unaligned` path. The alignment sweep still runs every pointer residue 0..8 off a 16-byte-aligned allocation (34 lengths x 9 offsets x 2 seeds each for 32 and 64) and additionally asserts alignment-invariance of the result.\n- `XXH_ACCEPT_NULL_INPUT_POINTER` is 0, so `XXH*_update(state, NULL, len)` returns `XXH_ERROR` for **every** len including 0, before touching the state. Tested for len in {0,1,2,15,16,17,31,32,100,1024,usize::MAX/2}, asserting the identical error code *and* that the state blob is bit-unchanged, *and* that the stream still produces the correct digest afterwards.\n- `LZ4_XXH32/64(NULL, 0, seed)` is defined (no dereference when len==0) and is tested against all seeds; `len != 0` with NULL would segfault and is only documented.\n- Skipped with an explicit comment block citing line numbers: `reset(NULL,·)`, `update(NULL, buf, len)`, `digest(NULL)`, `copyState(NULL,·)` — xxhash.c has **no** NULL check on the *state* pointer (the check guards `input`), so all four are guaranteed segfaults in the C ground truth.\n- `freeState(NULL)` is tested (returns `XXH_OK`).\n- **State layouts are identical** (`sizeof` 48 / 88, verified by compiling a probe against `xxhash.h`), so raw blobs *are* byte-compared after every reset/update/copyState. Because `reset` deliberately copies only `sizeof(state) - sizeof(reserved)` (44 / 80) bytes and `createState` is a bare `malloc`, every state is pre-filled with the same `0xAA` sentinel in both libraries; the tests additionally assert the `reserved` tail is left untouched by both implementations.\n\nUnrelated: `cargo test` on the whole workspace shows `tests/lz4_errors.rs` at 19 passed / 1 failed. That file is not mine and predates/parallels this work — my target is green."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1172,"cache_read_input_tokens":144759,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1172,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01HhghTocUcLcToJsT8dsA6c","session_id":"5bf83431-1376-4c81-bcb4-7fd267eb113b","uuid":"90514dc6-21fd-4eaf-a369-d40934baf6b9","timestamp":"2026-08-23T08:23:52.112Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write xxhash differential tests"}
