{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_cusn5zpgb53ksnftirlm63kd6lxfqxme25agegidp3ism3kh77fa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01GNFtggLV69PRwqhrxVxHXr","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'EOF'\np='tests/lz4frame_stream_diff.rs'\ns=open(p).read()\n\n# 1. Add a slack region so an over-write lands inside our own allocation.\nold_prep = \"\"\"    fn prep(&mut self, cap: usize) {\n        if self.cbuf.len() < cap {\n            self.cbuf.resize(cap, SENTINEL);\n            self.rbuf.resize(cap, SENTINEL);\n        }\n        self.cbuf[..cap].fill(SENTINEL);\n        self.rbuf[..cap].fill(SENTINEL);\n    }\"\"\"\nnew_prep = \"\"\"    /// Extra bytes allocated BEYOND the `dstCapacity` we hand to the library.\n    ///\n    /// `LZ4F_compressUpdateImpl` checks `dstCapacity` BEFORE calling\n    /// `LZ4F_flush()` on a blockCompressMode switch and then advances `dstPtr`\n    /// by the flushed byte count WITHOUT deducting it from the remaining budget\n    /// (lz4frame.c:1006-1016). So on a compressed->uncompressed switch the C\n    /// itself can write — and report — MORE than `dstCapacity`; observed:\n    /// `LZ4F_uncompressedUpdate(srcSize=65536, dstCapacity=65560) == 65574`.\n    /// Without slack that is a heap overflow inside the C library, which aborts\n    /// the process before any comparison can happen. With slack, the overrun\n    /// lands in our own allocation and stays fully comparable between C and Rust.\n    const SLACK: usize = 1 << 17;\n\n    fn prep(&mut self, cap: usize) {\n        let total = cap + Self::SLACK;\n        if self.cbuf.len() < total {\n            self.cbuf.resize(total, SENTINEL);\n            self.rbuf.resize(total, SENTINEL);\n        }\n        self.cbuf[..total].fill(SENTINEL);\n        self.rbuf[..total].fill(SENTINEL);\n    }\"\"\"\nassert old_prep in s\ns = s.replace(old_prep, new_prep)\n\n# 2. Compare the slack region too, so any divergence in the overrun is caught.\nold_cmp = \"\"\"    fn cmp_dst(&self, cap: usize, ctx: &str) {\n        assert_bytes_eq(ctx, &self.cbuf[..cap], &self.rbuf[..cap]);\n    }\"\"\"\nnew_cmp = \"\"\"    fn cmp_dst(&self, cap: usize, ctx: &str) {\n        // Compare the usable region AND the slack region: both sides were\n        // pre-filled with the same sentinel, so this also detects a divergence\n        // in how far past `dstCapacity` each implementation wrote.\n        let total = cap + Self::SLACK;\n        assert_bytes_eq(ctx, &self.cbuf[..total], &self.rbuf[..total]);\n    }\"\"\"\nassert old_cmp in s\ns = s.replace(old_cmp, new_cmp)\n\n# 3. The C legitimately returns > cap on the mode-switch path, so replace the\n#    hard assertion with the differential requirement plus a bound on slack.\nold_a = \"\"\"            assert!(cn <= cap, \"{}: returned {} > capacity {}\", label, cn, cap);\n            self.take(cn);\"\"\"\nnew_a = \"\"\"            // NOTE: `cn > cap` is REACHABLE in the C on a blockCompressMode\n            // switch (see `Sess::SLACK`). We therefore only require that C and\n            // Rust report the SAME count (already asserted by `same_ret`) and\n            // that it stays inside our slack-padded allocation.\n            assert!(\n                cn <= cap + Self::SLACK,\n                \"{}: returned {} beyond capacity {} + slack {}\",\n                label, cn, cap, Self::SLACK\n            );\n            self.take(cn);\"\"\"\nn = s.count(old_a)\nassert n == 2, n\ns = s.replace(old_a, new_a)\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nprint(\"patched prep/cmp_dst and\", n, \"capacity assertions\")\nEOF\ntimeout 600 cargo test --release --offline --test lz4frame_stream_diff -- --test-threads=1 2>&1 | grep -E '^test |test result|panicked at' | tail -25","description":"Apply slack fix and rerun stream tests"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":734,"cache_read_input_tokens":302465,