{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_nlomcpo3g7nh3c72femvqe5hx56fpxja5wpc4iqh3j3nmr5qbnmq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_015G7Hmbp6ejDB55gnyNvc2b","name":"Agent","input":{"subagent_type":"general-purpose","description":"Phase C row tests for lz4file","prompt":"Write row-by-row Phase C error-path differential tests for lz4file.c.\n\nWorking dir: /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-ieCE1Y/translated_rust\n\n## Your exact job\n`ERRORS.md` in the crate root is the ERROR-SURFACE TABLE, derived mechanically from the C\nsource. **Rows 222–250 are the `## lz4file.c` section and they are YOUR assignment.**\nRead those 29 rows. For EVERY ONE, write a test that constructs that exact invalid\ninput/condition, calls BOTH the C `.so` and the Rust `.so`, and asserts they return the\nSAME error code / sentinel — not merely \"both failed somehow\". For LZ4F functions that\nmeans asserting the identical `LZ4F_getErrorCode()` value, e.g. `err::ERROR_parameter_null`\n(21), `err::ERROR_io_read` (23), `err::ERROR_io_write` (22) — not just\n`lz4f_is_error(x) == true`.\n\nDeliverable: ONE new file `tests/lz4file_errors.rs`.\n\nDo NOT edit any other test file. If you find a genuine C-vs-Rust divergence, fix\n`src/lz4file.rs` (the C is ALWAYS correct). DO NOT MODIFY ANYTHING IN `c_src/`.\n\n## Context\n- C ground truth: `c_src/src/lz4file.c`, `c_src/include/lz4file.h` (only 341 lines of C —\n  read all of it)\n- Rust: `src/lz4file.rs`\n- Built libs: C = `c_src/build/liblz4.so`, Rust = `target/release/liblz4.so`\n- The 6 exported symbols: `LZ4F_writeOpen`, `LZ4F_write`, `LZ4F_writeClose`,\n  `LZ4F_readOpen`, `LZ4F_read`, `LZ4F_readClose`\n\n## MANDATORY harness\nREAD `tests/common/mod.rs` first. Read `tests/lz4_errors.rs` — it is the EXACT model for\nwhat I want, including how it documents rows it deliberately does not test. Also read the\nexisting `tests/lz4file_diff.rs` so you don't duplicate it (but DO still write a\nrow-labelled test per row; cross-reference the existing test by name in a comment if it\nalready covers that row).\n\nHarness API:\n- `both::<FnType>(\"symbol\") -> (c_fn, rust_fn)` — the ONLY way to obtain functions.\n  NEVER call Rust code directly, never `use lz4::...`.\n- `Rng::new(seed)`, `gen_shape`, `N_SHAPES`, `shape_name`, `assert_bytes_eq`\n- `LZ4F_preferences_t` (Default == LZ4F_INIT_PREFERENCES) and all `LZ4F_*` constants\n- `lz4f_is_error(code)`, `lz4f_error_code(code)`, `err::*`\n- **HARNESS RULE**: pre-fill the C buffer and the Rust buffer with the SAME sentinel byte\n  (`0xAA`) and compare the FULL buffer.\n- Write hex literals carefully: `0xF1LL`, `0x1EVE_1234` etc. are INVALID Rust.\n\nYou will need real `FILE*` handles. Declare libc `fopen`/`fclose`/`fflush`/`rewind`/\n`freopen`/`fileno`/`ftruncate` yourself with `extern \"C\"` — that is fine, they are libc,\nnot the library under test. `tests/lz4file_diff.rs` already does this; follow its\napproach. Put temp files under the directory from the `TMPDIR` env var with names unique\nper test, and clean them up.\n\n## How to reach the io_read / io_write error rows\nThese need a FILE* that legitimately fails:\n- **io_write**: open a file in READ-ONLY mode (`\"rb\"`) and pass it to `LZ4F_writeOpen` /\n  `LZ4F_write` so `fwrite` fails. Alternatively write to a full/closed stream. Find\n  whichever the C actually detects and mirror it.\n- **io_read**: a file shorter than `LZ4F_HEADER_SIZE_MAX` (19) makes `LZ4F_readOpen` fail\n  with `io_read`, because it does an unconditional `fread(buf,1,19,fp)` (already\n  established). Also try a file opened WRITE-ONLY (`\"wb\"`) and passed to `LZ4F_readOpen`.\n- Note ERRORS.md flags that `LZ4F_read`'s own `RETURN_ERROR(io_read)` is **dead code**\n  because `ret` is `size_t`, so a failed `fread` is indistinguishable from EOF and takes\n  the `break`. If a row is unreachable, say so with the line number rather than faking it.\n- ERRORS.md also flags that `LZ4F_writeClose` returns `LZ4F_OK_NoError` (0) even when a\n  prior `LZ4F_write` latched an error. Test that error-masking behaviour explicitly.\n\nCover the obvious boundaries too: NULL `FILE*`, NULL state pointer, NULL out-pointer,\nsize 0, huge sizes, calling `LZ4F_write` after `LZ4F_writeClose`, `LZ4F_read` after\n`LZ4F_readClose`, double-close, and `LZ4F_readClose(NULL)` / `LZ4F_writeClose(NULL)` if\nthe C tolerates them (check first — if it would segfault, document instead of testing).\n\n## Naming and traceability — REQUIRED\nName each test so the row is obvious, e.g. `row_222_write_open_null_fp`. Group closely\nrelated rows in one `#[test]` when natural, but the function name and inline comments MUST\ncite every row number covered. At the END of the file add a comment block listing EVERY\nrow 222–250 with either its covering test function or the precise reason it cannot be\ntested. Valid reasons (and ONLY these):\n- the branch is dead/unreachable in the C (give the line number and why)\n- the code is not compiled into the library under test\n- the C documents it as `assert`-only / undefined behaviour (say which assert + line)\n- compile-time `#error` / static assert\nDo NOT use \"hard to test\" as a reason. Allocation-failure rows ARE testable if the API\nexposes an allocator hook; if `lz4file.c` uses plain `calloc`/`malloc` with no hook, say\nso explicitly with the line number.\n\n## Make it pass\n```\ncd /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-ieCE1Y/translated_rust\ntimeout 600 cargo test --release --offline --test lz4file_errors 2>&1 | tail -40\n```\nAlways `--release --offline`. Other agents may build concurrently — if cargo blocks on the\ntarget lock, wait and retry. After ANY edit to `src/lz4file.rs` run\n`timeout 600 cargo build --release --offline` before re-running, then re-run the existing\nlz4file suite: `timeout 600 cargo test --release --offline --test lz4file_diff`.\nKeep runtime under ~90 s.\n\nReport back: the exact `#[test